{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And it shall be, because you heed these ordinances.</b> Rashi explained: if the light commandments that people trample with their heels you will heed. He did not wish to interpret ״עֵקֶב״ here as the language of ״עֵקֶב אֲשֶׁר שָׁמַע אַבְרָהָם בְּקוֹלִי״ (Genesis 26:5), because it was difficult for him: behold, in the preceding verse it says ״וּשְׁמַרְתֶּם אֶת הַחֻקִּים וְאֶת הַמִּשְׁפָּטִים״ — so why did he mention here מִּשְׁפָּטִים without חֻקִּים? Rather it is certain that the expression ״עֵקֶב״ includes the חֻקִּים, and he alluded to them through the expression ״עֵקֶב,״ because the Satan and the nations scorn them on the grounds that their reason is not known, and on account of this people treat them lightly and trample them with the heel. And this is what he said: if the light commandments, etc. Therefore he mentioned them with the expression ״עֵקֶב״ and not with the expression חֻקִּים, to tell you that the חֻקִּים concerning which I warned you above — perhaps, because people trample them with their heels, you will not be careful and zealous in them as in the מִּשְׁפָּטִים and laws whose benefit is open and known to you. Therefore I say that even the חֻקִּים alluded to in ״עֵקֶב,״ although people trample them with their heels, nevertheless you shall be careful in them as in the מִּשְׁפָּטִים. And ״אֵת״ is the expression ״עִם,״ as is its rule in many places, where the word ״אֵת״ conveys the sense of ״עִם,״ as if he said: that you should heed the חֻקִּים together with the מִּשְׁפָּטִים, and these two divisions of commandment shall be equal in your eyes in every respect. For just as you are careful regarding the מִּשְׁפָּטִים, so shall you be careful regarding the חֻקִּים — and conversely, in a manner that will be explained below." "And a proof for our words from what is brought in the Yalkut (§446): ״וְהָיָה עֵקֶב תִּשְׁמְעוּן״ — this is what is said (Psalms 49:6): ״לָמָּה אִירָא בִּימֵי רָע עֲוֹן עֲקֵבַי יְסוּבֵּנִי.״ David said: I do not fear the severe commandments because they are severe; but I fear the light ones, lest I have transgressed them. And You said: be careful in a light commandment as in a severe one. Therefore it says: ״וְהָיָה עֵקֶב תִּשְׁמְעוּן.״ David said before the Holy One, blessed be He: ״גַּם עַבְדְּךָ נִזְהָר בָּהֶם, בְּשָׁמְרָם עֵקֶב רָב״ (ibid. 19:12). ״מָה רַב טוּבְךָ״ (ibid. 31:20) — this is the reward of the light commandments. End of his language.\n\nI searched through all of Scripture and did not find that the Holy One, blessed be He, said: be careful in a light commandment as in a severe one. So how could David say: and You said, etc.? And how did the author of the midrash attach these words of his to the verse ״וְהָיָה עֵקֶב תִּשְׁמְעוּן״? And what indication is there that the verse ״מָה רַב טוּבְךָ״ speaks of the reward of the light commandments?\n\nRather, it is certainly because it was difficult for him: ״וְהָיָה עֵקֶב תִּשְׁמְעוּן אֵת הַמִּשְׁפָּטִים״ — why did he not mention also the חֻקִּים? And furthermore, if the word ״עֵקֶב״ alludes to the light commandments, he should have said ״וְאֵת הַמִּשְׁפָּטִים,״ for the word ״אֵת״ would not be connected to what precedes it." "Rather, it is certain that ״עֵקֶב״ refers to the חֻקִּים — and these are the commandments that are light in the eyes of the multitudes. And the word ״אֵת״ means ״עִם,״ and this is that which he said: be careful in a light commandment as in a severe one — by predicating the חֻקִּים upon the מִּשְׁפָּטִים, in saying: that you should heed the חֻקִּים together with the מִּשְׁפָּטִים, equal and comparable to them. And on this he expounded the verse ״מָה רַב טוּבְךָ אֲשֶׁר צָפַנְתָּ לִּירֵאֶיךָ, פָּעַלְתָּ לַחוֹסִים בָּךְ נֶגֶד בְּנֵי אָדָם״ — that is to say: one who fulfills the חֻקִּים, this is a matter that is ״נֶגֶד בְּנֵי אָדָם,״ for it is opposed to the majority of the children of man, for as they are numerous, so do they scorn them. And one who fulfills them certainly expects nothing but the reward of the World to Come, for in this world their benefit is not revealed as is the benefit of the מִּשְׁפָּטִים revealed to all. And one who fulfills them takes refuge in Him, blessed be He, that He will not diminish his reward. Therefore it says: ״פָּעַלְתָּ לַחוֹסִים בָּךְ נֶגֶד בְּנֵי אָדָם״ — they take refuge in You in a matter that is opposed to the children of man who scorn them. And on this he cites: ״גַּם עַבְדְּךָ נִזְהָר בָּהֶם, בְּשָׁמְרָם עֵקֶב רָב,״ and ״נִזְהָר״ is an expression of brightness and clarity, for through the observance of the חֻקִּים alluded to in ״עֵקֶב,״ those who understand shall shine bright as the brightness of the firmament in the World to Come. This is what is said here: ״וְשָׁמַר ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ לְךָ אֶת הַבְּרִית״ etc.; the Yalkut (§447) concludes: that He will keep your reward for you for the World to Come." "And this is what we said — that what he said about equating the חֻקִּים to the מִּשְׁפָּטִים is in two respects. The first is what we already said regarding zeal: just as you are zealous in the performance of the מִּשְׁפָּטִים because in your eyes you see the benefit, so shall you be careful also in the חֻקִּים alluded to in ״עֵקֶב.״ And conversely: just as with the חֻקִּים, since their benefit is not felt by you, you surely fulfill them for the sake of Heaven and not for your own enjoyment — for the enjoyment is not felt by you at the time — so too the מִּשְׁפָּטִים you shall not fulfill for the sake of your enjoyment, but rather because the commandment of YHWH is upon you, as it is written (Psalms 119:101–102): ״מִכָּל אֹרַח רָע כָּלִאתִי רַגְלָי לְמַעַן אֶשְׁמֹר דְּבָרֶךָ, מִמִּשְׁפָּטֶיךָ לֹא סָרְתִּי כִּי אַתָּה הוֹרֵתָנִי״ — just as the חֻקִּים that I fulfill, surely my intention is only לְמַעַן אֶשְׁמֹר חֻקֶּיךָ, statutes that You have decreed for me, so from מִּשְׁפָּטֶיךָ I have not turned aside — not for the sake of my felt benefit, but rather because אַתָּה הוֹרֵתָנִי. And along these lines our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Tanchuma Kedoshim 28): let no person say, it is impossible for me to eat pork, etc. — meaning, let him not say that the only reason he does not eat is that it is impossible, but rather that he fulfills even what his soul desires because You commanded it." "And this is what is said at the end of parashat Va'etchanan: ״וּמְשַׁלֵּם לְשׂוֹנְאָיו אֶל פָּנָיו לְהַאֲבִידוֹ, לֹא יְאַחֵר לְשׂוֹנְאוֹ אֶל פָּנָיו יְשַׁלֶּם לוֹ. וְשָׁמַרְתָּ אֶת הַמִּצְוָה וְאֶת הַחֻקִּים וְאֶת הַמִּשְׁפָּטִים אֲשֶׁר אָנֹכִי מְצַוְּךָ הַיּוֹם לַעֲשׂוֹתָם.״ Rashi explained: in his lifetime He repays him his good reward in order to drive him out of the World to Come. And it is difficult: if the gentile fulfills a certain commandment, why does Scripture call him ״שׂוֹנְאוֹ״ — His enemy — at the time of the fulfillment of the commandment? Rather, it is certainly because the gentile, even at the time he fulfills a certain commandment from among the rational ones, does so only for the sake of his own felt enjoyment at the time. Therefore his law is, as he himself has ruled, that he receives his reward in this world. But you shall not do so to YHWH your Elohim, but rather ״וְשָׁמַרְתָּ״ etc. ״אֲשֶׁר אָנֹכִי מְצַוְּךָ״ — that is to say: because I have commanded you, you shall observe them, and not for the sake of your enjoyment. And then, automatically, the מִּשְׁפָּטִים and the חֻקִּים will be equal in your eyes. And it is concerning this that he drew the inference: ״וְהָיָה עֵקֶב תִּשְׁמְעוּן אֵת הַמִּשְׁפָּטִים״ — and then it is fitting that the principal reward be kept for you for the World to Come: ״וְשָׁמַר ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ לְךָ״ etc." "And this is what is said at the end of the parashah: ״כַּגּוֹיִם אֲשֶׁר ה׳ מַאֲבִיד מִפְּנֵיכֶם כֵּן תֹּאבֵדוּן עֵקֶב לֹא תִשְׁמְעוּן בְּקוֹל ה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם״, and this entire verse is superfluous, for it was already said earlier: ״כִּי אָבֹד תֹּאבֵדוּן״. And furthermore, why did he suspend their destruction upon the destruction of the nations? Rather, surely this verse is not connected at all with what is above, for above he spoke of idolatry: ״וְהָיָה אִם שָׁכֹחַ תִּשְׁכַּח״ etc., and concerning idolatry he said ״כִּי אָבֹד תֹּאבֵדוּן״ — he doubled the destruction to say that one who worships idolatry is banished both from this world and from the world to come. And afterward he said that there is another destruction which is from the world to come alone, and that is one who does not fulfill the ״עֵקֶב״ alluding to the ḥukkim, because their benefit is not revealed for the moment, and then presumably even what he does fulfill of the commandments he fulfills only for the sake of momentary pleasure. And concerning him he said: ״כַּגּוֹיִם אֲשֶׁר ה׳ מַאֲבִיד״ — and that is the destruction mentioned above, ״וּמְשַׁלֵּם לְשׂוֹנְאָיו אֶל פָּנָיו לְהַאֲבִידוֹ״ — from the world to come, ״כֵּן תֹּאבֵדוּן״ — from the world to come. And he gave the reason for the matter and said: ״עֵקֶב לֹא תִשְׁמְעוּן״ — because you will not hear the commandments alluded to in ״עֵקֶב״; this implies that one who fulfills what is alluded to in ״עֵקֶב״ eats of their fruit in this world and the principal remains stored for him for the world to come. Therefore it says here: ״וְשָׁמַר ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ לְךָ אֶת הַבְּרִית וְאֶת הַחֶסֶד״, and this is a precious interpretation." "<b>And HaShem your Elohim will keep for you the covenant etc.</b> By the word ״לְךָ״ he wished to resolve what is said, ״אֲשֶׁר נִשְׁבַּע לַאֲבֹתֶיךָ״ — but was not the oath unconditional, so that even if they do not merit, would HaShem not fulfill the oath on that account? Rather, if you do not merit, then HaShem will keep the oath, but not for you — rather for another generation; or in the manner He said to Moses, ״וְאֶעֱשֶׂה אוֹתְךָ לְגוֹי גָּדוֹל״ (Exodus 32:10). And if you merit — He will keep His promise to you for this generation. And the fact that it says ״לְךָ״ as though speaking to an individual, even though he began as though speaking to the multitude — nevertheless he mentioned ״לְךָ״ to allude also to the reward of the world to come, where they make a dwelling for each righteous person individually." "And He said, <b>blessed shall you be among all the peoples.</b> In Rav [commentary] he interpreted that all the peoples shall bless you etc., in the manner of the evil angel answering amen. And the MaharI'A raised a difficulty against this and said: what advantage is there for Israel in this? And I say: because the land of Israel — the eyes of HaShem seek it first, and through that seeking He seeks all the other lands, for all the lands drink from the distillate of the land of Israel; therefore all the peoples will necessarily bless Israel, for in their peace there will be peace for them as well. And as it is written (Psalms 117:1–2): ״הַלְלוּ אֶת ה׳ כָּל גּוֹיִם שַׁבְּחוּהוּ כָּל הָאֻמִּים, כִּי גָבַר עָלֵינוּ חַסְדּוֹ״. And therefore ״וְאָכַלְתָּ אֶת כָּל הָעַמִּים״ — for from what is yours they will give to you. And specifically ״אֲשֶׁר ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ נוֹתֵן לָךְ״ — those delivered into your hand, as it is written ״כִּי לַחְמֵנוּ הֵם״ (Numbers 14:9), and not those that are not delivered into your hand, for robbery from a gentile is forbidden."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {5 "The verse quoted from the end of the parashah (Deuteronomy 8:20) and the phrase וּמְשַׁלֵּם לְשׂוֹנְאָיו אֶל פָּנָיו לְהַאֲבִידוֹ are from Deuteronomy 7:10; the Kli Yakar cites them without explicit verse addresses.", 7 "בְּרַבָּתִי appears to refer to a midrashic or rabbinic source; resolved as 'In Rav [commentary]' but the precise reference is uncertain. מהרי״ן is read as מהרי״א per the user's text."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:12:35.649525288Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}